\section{What resume does not do}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{A page reload starts fresh}, as without resume. Resume is for a page
that is still open.
\item \strong{An unhandled error in an observer ends the session}, and its saved
state is deleted. So does \code{session$close()}. The page is not retried.
\item \strong{Saved state that no longer fits is dropped.} If, once the server
function has run, a labelled source has no saved value (and was not
left out with \code{\link[=excludeFromResume]{excludeFromResume()}}) or two sources share a label they
did not share when the state was saved, the session closes and the page
reconnects at once, resuming from its inputs only. The server function
runs again, as it would on a reload. Without \code{appVersion}, a change to
the app's R files makes sessions resume from their inputs only instead,
before anything runs. The browser then shows "Reconnected, but your
previous work could not be restored.", as it does whenever a page that
had saved state resumes from its inputs only; call \code{event.preventDefault()}
in a \code{shiny:resumed} handler to suppress it.
\item Structure an \code{observeEvent()} handler created after startup (an output,
a module server, an observer) does not come back on its own, because
that handler does not run again. Rebuild it in \code{session$onResume()};
see rule 3.
\item Closure variables (assigned with \verb{<<-}), reference objects and async
work in flight are never saved. A handler that assigns closure
variables is held back like any other, so they keep their initial
values.
\item \strong{A drop during a resume loses the saved state.} A record is used
once, and the resumed session saves its own after its first flush; if
the connection drops or the process stops in between, the next attempt
resumes from inputs only.
}
}

\section{Who decides}{

Resolved once per app, first match wins:
\enumerate{
\item The app's last \code{enableResume()} call, with \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}.
\item The \code{SHINY_RESUME} environment variable (\code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}), for a
hosting platform that has verified its storage.
\item Off.
}
}

\section{What is saved}{

Every \code{reactiveVal()} and \code{reactiveValues()} with a label: an explicit
\code{label} (\code{.label} for \code{reactiveValues()}), or the name it is assigned to
(\code{count <- reactiveVal(0)}), in app code and in installed packages alike.
A source with no label (one created in \code{lapply()}, or by a helper
function) is not saved and after a resume holds its initial value; in
development mode (\code{\link[=devmode]{devmode()}}) Shiny names it. A value that cannot be
serialized (a connection, an R6 object, a closure), one over
\code{maxNodeSize}, or two sources sharing a label when the state is saved
block saved state for the whole session: it is restored all or nothing,
so the session resumes from its inputs only and the log says why.
\code{session$userData} is saved best-effort instead: an entry that cannot be
saved is left out and reported, and the rest comes back. Sources are all or
nothing because restoring some and not others can leave the app in a state
it was never designed for.
\code{session$resumeReport()} lists every value with the reason it would not
restore.

Values that commonly block resume because they carry an environment or a
function: model fits and formulas created inside a function, ggplot
objects, S7 objects, and anything holding a connection, an external
pointer or an R6 object. Keep them in a \code{\link[=reactive]{reactive()}}, which recomputes
after a resume, or leave the source out with \code{\link[=excludeFromResume]{excludeFromResume()}} and
rebuild the value in \code{session$onResume()}.
}

\section{Rebuilding on resume}{

\code{session$onResume(fun)} registers a function that runs once when a page
reconnects, after the server function and before any observer or output
runs. By then saved values are restored, or, if saved state could not be
used, the session started from its inputs only and every source holds its
initial value. \code{fun} takes one argument, \code{info}, a list
whose \code{restored} element is \code{TRUE} when saved state was restored and
\code{FALSE} when the session resumed from its inputs only. It also fires with
resume off, when \code{session$allowReconnect(TRUE)} reconnects a page, with
\code{restored} \code{FALSE}. It never fires on a fresh start.

The function runs inside \code{\link[=isolate]{isolate()}}, so it can read reactive values
directly. Registered in a module, it runs in that module's namespace.
Module servers it starts may register their own, which run in the same
pass. An error in it ends the session.

Panels added by clicks come back live after a restart:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{ids <- reactiveVal(character())
observeEvent(input$add, \{
  id <- paste0("p", input$add)
  ids(c(ids(), id))
  insertUI("#panels", ui = panelUI(id))
  panelServer(id)
\})
session$onResume(function(info) \{
  for (id in ids()) panelServer(id)
\})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{ids} comes back with the saved state; the handler is held back; the
callback starts each panel's server, whose own values are restored as it
creates them. The panels themselves are still on the page.
}

\section{The rules of reactives}{

Four habits keep resumed sessions complete and correct. In development
mode Shiny names the rule a session breaks.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Label sources created in loops or through helpers.} A source made
in \code{lapply()} or by a wrapper function has no name to infer. Give it
one (\code{reactiveVal(0, label = paste0("count", i))}) or leave it out with
\code{\link[=excludeFromResume]{excludeFromResume()}}.
\item \strong{Hold reference objects in a \code{reactive()}, not a \code{reactiveVal()}.} A
connection, pool or R6 client cannot be saved; in a \code{reactive()} it is
rebuilt on first read. If one must live in a source, leave the source
out with \code{\link[=excludeFromResume]{excludeFromResume()}} and rebuild the object in
\code{session$onResume()}.
\item \strong{Build structure from state.} A handler that defines an output,
starts a module server or creates an observer is held back on resume,
so what it built is gone; the log names outputs on the page that have
no definition. Either derive the structure from state (\code{\link[=renderUI]{renderUI()}},
an \code{\link[=observe]{observe()}} over ids), or keep the ids in a \code{reactiveVal()} and
rebuild in \code{session$onResume()}, as above. For a handler that builds
one output from inputs, prefer \code{\link[=eventReactive]{eventReactive()}}:
\code{n <- eventReactive(input$go, input$n)} with
\code{output$plot <- renderPlot(make_plot(n()))} needs nothing on resume. It
recomputes from the current inputs; when the committed value matters
(the \code{n} the user clicked Go with, not where the slider is now), store
it in a \code{reactiveVal()} from \code{observeEvent()}.
\item \strong{Accumulate into a reactive value from \code{observeEvent()}, not a plain
\code{observe()}.} A plain observer has no event to hold back, so it runs
on resume and applies its change again:
\code{observe({ input$plus; isolate(count(count() + 1)) })} shows 4 after
three clicks and a resume; \code{observeEvent(input$plus, count(count() + 1))}
shows 3.
The same goes for an observer used as a handler:
\code{observe({ req(input$go); send() })} runs on resume and sends again,
because a button's count comes back non-zero; write
\code{observeEvent(input$go, send())}. In development mode Shiny names such
observers.
}

On resume, inputs the previous session already had are restored, never
replayed: no handler fires for them. Changes made while the page was
disconnected are delivered once each, in order, after the first render,
and fire their handlers as usual. One edge: a change the old session never
received counts as already handled and is not replayed. That covers
changes made between the last message the server processed and the moment
the browser noticed the connection was gone (after a silent network loss
this can be a long time), and changes sent on a reconnect attempt that
opened but was not answered. Design side effects so that losing one such
event is harmless, rather than relying on exactly-once delivery.

Also: \code{\link[=bindCache]{bindCache()}} with a persistent cache keeps expensive reactive
expressions cheap after a restart; \code{observeEvent(once = TRUE)} may fire once more after a
resume; process-scoped values (temp paths, process ids) serialize fine
and are wrong after a restart; an unconditional top-level
\code{session$userData$x <- 0} overwrites the saved value, so guard it with
\verb{if (is.null(session$userData$x))} or use a \code{reactiveVal()}. Closure
variables are never saved and the handlers that assign them are held
back; in development mode Shiny names them. \code{session$onResume()} also
fires with resume off, which is how an app that only calls
\code{session$allowReconnect(TRUE)} redoes what a held-back handler built:
\code{session$onResume(function(info) if (input$go > 0) output$plot <- renderPlot(...))}.
}

\section{What the log says}{

Each resume writes one line to standard error: \verb{Resumed: restored N values; K event handlers held back}, or \verb{Resumed from inputs only: <cause>}, plus \verb{Outputs on the page with no definition after resume: ...} when rule 3 is broken. A resumed session also logs \verb{Saved values not restored because nothing recreated them: ...} when saved values
had no source to go to by the end of the first flush (rule 3), and a
session whose saved state does not fit logs \verb{Resume failed after the server function ran: ... The session restarted from inputs only.}
}

\section{What is saved, and where}{

With resume on, a record of the session is written shortly after each
change and when the connection closes. It holds every labelled
\code{reactiveVal()} and \code{reactiveValues()}, \code{session$userData} (minus
\code{userDataExclude}) and copies of uploaded files. Input values are not
saved: after a reconnect they come from the browser, which kept them.
Values marked with \code{\link[=excludeFromResume]{excludeFromResume()}} are never written.

Records live in the first of these that is set: the \code{SHINY_RESUME_DIR}
environment variable (an operator's or hosting platform's explicit
choice); on Posit Connect, a \code{shiny-resume} folder inside the content's
persistent data directory (\code{CONNECT_CONTENT_DATA_DIR}, set when
persistent data directories are enabled); otherwise under
\code{tools::R_user_dir("shiny", "cache")}. Never in the app directory. Each
app's records go in their own subdirectory, named by a hash of the app
id: the \code{SHINY_RESUME_APP_ID} environment variable when set, else
\code{CONNECT_CONTENT_GUID} on Posit Connect (unlike the bundle directory, it
stays the same across deploys), else the app's directory. The directory
and each record are made readable only by the owner. A record is keyed
by a 128-bit random token and the app id, kept for 24 hours, and deleted
when the page is closed, when the session ends for good, or when a resume
reads it. Each app's records are capped at 1 GiB and 1000 in all; copies
of uploaded files are not counted towards the 1 GiB and are deleted with
their record. Nothing is written unless the app called \code{enableResume()}
or the platform set \code{SHINY_RESUME}. On Windows the token comes from the
\code{openssl} package when it is installed and from R's random number
generator otherwise.

A store is only as shared as its directory. On Connect, the persistent
data directory survives worker restarts and, on shared storage, resumes
across machines. Elsewhere the store is per machine: behind a load
balancer without sticky sessions a reconnect that lands on another
machine resumes from inputs only, and a container without a persistent
volume does the same after it restarts. Shiny already needs sticky
sessions for its WebSocket, so this adds no new deployment requirement.
}

\examples{
\dontrun{
# app.R
library(shiny)
enableResume(appVersion = "2")

ui <- fluidPage(actionButton("plus", "+1"), textOutput("count"))
server <- function(input, output, session) {
  count <- reactiveVal(0)
  observeEvent(input$plus, count(count() + 1))

  # A connection cannot be saved: keep it in a reactive(), which recomputes.
  conn <- reactive(file(tempfile(), open = "w"))

  output$count <- renderText(count())
}
shinyApp(ui, server)
}
}
